GPAC / libgpac
==============

Eclipsa Studio's "Export to MP4" feature uses GPAC (libgpac) to mux the
IAMF audio bitstream together with the session video into a single
IAMF-in-ISOBMFF .mp4 file (engine/iamf/iamf_muxer.cc).

  Project:    GPAC — https://gpac.io  (https://github.com/gpac/gpac)
  Version:    commit 9edee648038ff542530b7a9f0392801ae88ea915 (26.08-DEV)
              plus the patches in engine/deps/gpac/patches/
  Copyright:  © Telecom Paris and the GPAC authors
  License:    GNU Lesser General Public License, version 2.1 or later
              (LGPL-2.1-or-later)

libgpac is linked DYNAMICALLY and shipped as a separate shared library
(libgpac.dylib on macOS, libgpac.dll on Windows) alongside the audio
engine. No part of GPAC is statically linked into Eclipsa Studio.

In accordance with section 6 of the LGPL v2.1, you may modify libgpac and
use a modified version with Eclipsa Studio: replace the bundled libgpac
(libgpac.dylib in "Eclipsa Studio.app/Contents/Frameworks" on macOS,
libgpac.dll beside the application executable on Windows) with your own
build of an interface-compatible libgpac and the application will load it.
The corresponding source for the bundled build is GPAC at the commit
listed above, available from https://github.com/gpac/gpac, with the
patches in engine/deps/gpac/patches/ applied (each is a git diff against
that commit; engine/deps/gpac/patches/README.md says what each changes).
The exact build configuration is recorded in
engine/deps/gpac/build_gpac.sh (macOS) and engine/deps/gpac/build_gpac.ps1
(Windows) — both a "--static-bin --use-curl=no" build, carrying no
FFmpeg / curl / OpenSSL dependency.

